# Carom — Menus, screens, and navigation

This file defines the game's screens, the menus, how a player moves between them,
the scoring and match flow that drives those transitions, and audio. Every figure
and every piece of screen copy below carries the name this specification gives
it.

## Scoring and match flow

- A point is scored when a ball fully passes a goal edge (`x < 0` or
  `x > FIELD_W`). The point goes to the player on the opposite side and increments
  their score.
- First to `WIN_SCORE` (`11`) points wins, and the winner leads by at least
  `WIN_LEAD` (`2`). At `10-10` play continues until one player is two points
  ahead. `state.winner` holds the winning side once a match is over, and is `null`
  until then.

## Screens and navigation

The game is a small state machine, and `state.screen` names the state it is in.
Each state has its own screen, and the player moves between them as below.

1. `title`, the main menu. It shows the title `TITLE_TEXT` (`CAROM`), the tagline
   `TAGLINE_TEXT` (`NEON PADDLE DUEL`), and a vertical menu of `TITLE_ITEMS`
   (`SOLO`, `VERSUS`, `HOW TO PLAY`) in that order. The item at `state.menuIndex`
   is highlighted, drawn distinctly from the others. The field furniture (paddles,
{{#if (eq variant.slug "multi")}}
   balls, obstacles, net) may show dimmed behind the menu. `SOLO` starts a
{{else}}
   ball, obstacles, net) may show dimmed behind the menu. `SOLO` starts a
{{/if}}
   single-player match; `VERSUS` starts a two-player match; `HOW TO PLAY` opens
   the how-to-play screen.
2. `howto`, how to play. It names the controls and describes the spin and obstacle
   mechanics. `back` returns to the title.
{{#if (eq variant.slug "multi")}}
3. `playing`, the live game: both paddles, the three balls each with its own
   trail, both obstacles, the net, the two scores near the top, and a small mode
   label.
4. `countdown`, the hold before the three balls launch at the start of a match,
   rendered over the live field with the same furniture. It becomes `playing`
   when they launch. A ball that takes its home point again during a rally holds
   and relaunches on its own, leaving the screen on `playing`.
5. `paused`, reachable from `countdown` or `playing`. It offers `PAUSE_ITEMS`
   (`RESUME`, `RESTART`, `QUIT TO MENU`) in that order. The field is visible and
   frozen behind the pause menu, with the balls, both paddles, and every hold
   timer all held still. `RESUME` returns to `state.resumeScreen`, the screen the
   pause interrupted; `RESTART` starts the same mode over; `QUIT TO MENU` returns
   to `title`.
{{else}}
3. `playing`, the live game: both paddles, the ball with its trail, both
   obstacles, the net, the two scores near the top, and a small mode label.
4. `countdown`, the pre-serve hold before a ball is launched, rendered over the
   live field with the same furniture. It becomes `playing` when the ball
   launches.
5. `paused`, reachable from `countdown` or `playing`. It offers `PAUSE_ITEMS`
   (`RESUME`, `RESTART`, `QUIT TO MENU`) in that order. The field is visible and
   frozen behind the pause menu, with the ball, both paddles, and the countdown
   all held still. `RESUME` returns to `state.resumeScreen`, the screen the pause
   interrupted; `RESTART` starts the same mode over; `QUIT TO MENU` returns to
   `title`.
{{/if}}
6. `matchover`, shown when a player wins. It displays the winning side and the
   final score, and offers `MATCHOVER_ITEMS` (`PLAY AGAIN`, `MENU`) in that order.

## Menu navigation

Keyboard only, through the same registered actions a match uses. On the title, the
how-to-play screen, the pause menu, and the match-over screen: either side's up
and down actions (`p1-up`/`p1-down` or `p2-up`/`p2-down`) move `state.menuIndex`,
`confirm` accepts the highlighted item, and `back` goes back. Those actions, and
the keys bound to them, are held in `ACTIONS` and `BINDINGS`.

## Audio

Audio plays through the runtime's cue bus. Define and play exactly the four cues
in `CUES`, under exactly these names, one per event:

| Cue | Constant | Played when |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `paddle-hit` | `CUES.paddleHit` | A ball strikes a paddle. |
| `wall-bounce` | `CUES.wallBounce` | A ball reflects off the top or bottom wall. |
| `obstacle-bounce` | `CUES.obstacleBounce` | A ball bounces off an obstacle. |
| `score` | `CUES.score` | A point is scored. |

Each is a distinct short blip, so the four events are told apart by ear. Each is
played on the frame its event happens, from `update`.

Muting and the first-interaction unlock belong to the runtime. The game binds the
`mute` action to the runtime's mute bit and toggles it from any screen, then
mirrors that bit into `state.muted` every frame. The game stays fully playable
with sound muted.

## Out of scope

- Network or online multiplayer.
- Touch or gamepad input. This version is keyboard only.
- Destructible obstacles. The obstacles persist for the whole match.
- Persistence of scores or settings between sessions.
